{"title":"2026 CEMC Cayley Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A Nelson card has a number on each side. Three Nelson cards lie on a table, as shown.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Jen flips the cards one by one, flipping the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and then the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Each time she flips a card, the mean (average) of the three visible numbers increases by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. What number is on the opposite side of the card with the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/d/a8da7a146462489986e5c70f7c432562c1bb80ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\   11 \\qquad \t\\textbf{(B)}\\   8 \\qquad \t\\textbf{(C)}\\   10 \\qquad \t\\textbf{(D)}\\   9 \\qquad \t\\textbf{(E)}\\   6 \\qquad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"402\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of the numbers on the front is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/f/c2fa753e8dadee092a9c89cecea2704361a9aa87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 + 7 + 8 = 10 + 8 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"190\" height=\"15\" />, so the average is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/b/b1be10b5fb87d5763e269b077381ab70258295d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{18}{3} = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"57\" height=\"38\" />. Since the average of the numbers increases by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for each card that is flipped, the new average after the card with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is flipped is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/8/0185369f72d784ecdc1e90563748260a24af8f7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 + 1 = 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" />, so the sum of the numbers so far would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/0/e5061878e47614e71d424253cc7d146ab3f0e64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 3 = 21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, the number on the other side of the card with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/8/638b86b2258570a5554c1c98143770b5bc9b2cd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$21 - (7 + 8) = 21 - 15 = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"213\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Repeating this, the new average after the card with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is flipped is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/1/7e15253865f049fe6187d1ba5c34a252766811c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 + 1 = 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />, so the sum of the numbers at that point would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/9/5598d118a4c157ca3d93f71c4529b35789a80fe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\times 3 = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, the number on the other side of the card with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/a/d9a82be455dafacb0aefd8c8287c682a858f4231.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24 - (6 + 8) = 24 - 14 = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"222\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, the new average after the card with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is flipped is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/2/6c201a2783343b2ba8fcbb08aa582a73953b05ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 + 1 = 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />, so the sum of the numbers after all cards are flipped is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/f/6ff2ae475e662eb83e408487daa5688a343d18bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 \\times 3 = 27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, the number on the other side of the card with an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/2/752e43d93b2c93dfe110faeb01c2663393469189.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27 - (6 + 10) = 27 - 16 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(A) } 11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"278\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Faster.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Faster)\">Solution 2 (Faster)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Faster)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can realize that for the average to increase by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the number on the back would have to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b4a9a68a4effe67f5ea0695524933ce7345cf39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 1 = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> more than the number on the other side because there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> numbers. Thus, the number on the opposite side of the card with an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb3ef3f2a50ef338efbcd8b2d54b689b2f918306.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 + 3 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(A) } 11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"132\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley\">2026 CEMC Cayley</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley Problems/Problem 9 (page does not exist)\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley Problems/Problem 11 (page does not exist)\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</